<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Louis Auguste Blanqui</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1805">1805</a>–<a href="/years/1881">1881</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Louis Auguste Blanqui was a French revolutionary socialist who spent more than thirty years of his life in prison. He held that a small, disciplined group of conspirators should seize power and set up a temporary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a> on behalf of the people, a doctrine known as Blanquism. Arrested just before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris-commune">Paris Commune</a> of 1871, he was elected to its council in his absence, and his followers formed one of its main factions.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/louis-auguste-blanqui.jpg" alt="Louis Auguste Blanqui" width="320" height="422" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Appert, Eugène (Châteauroux, 13–03–1830), photographe · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Blanqui_Louis-Auguste_(1805-1881)._R%C3%A9volutionnaire_et_activiste,_il_est_de_toutes_les_manifestations_qui_pr%C3%B4ne_l%27_%C3%A9gal,_PH4142-49_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Louis-Auguste Blanqui was born on 8 February 1805 in Puget-Théniers, in south-east <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. His father, Jean Dominique Blanqui, had been a Girondist deputy in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-convention">National Convention</a>. At 13 he was sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> to study, and he graduated from the Lycée Charlemagne with honours in 1824. In 1822 he witnessed the execution of the four sergeants of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-rochelle">La Rochelle</a>, which he later cited as the origin of his declaration of war upon all factions that represent the past. He joined the Carbonari in 1824 and was wounded in the protests of 1827 against Charles X, after which he moved from Bonapartism to Jacobin republicanism.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Blanqui joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/july-revolution">July Revolution</a> of 1830 but was disillusioned when it replaced one monarchy with another. At his trial in 1832 he declared his profession to be proletarian. Influenced by Philippe Buonarroti, he turned to disciplined conspiracy, founding the Société des Familles in 1834 and later the Société des Saisons. On 12 May 1839 he led it in an insurrection in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> that was crushed after two days. Arrested in January 1840 and sentenced to death, commuted to life in prison, he spent years at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mont-saint-michel">Mont-Saint-Michel</a>.</p><p>Released shortly before the Revolution of 1848, he founded the Société républicaine centrale and demanded the red flag in place of the tricolor. He was arrested after the demonstration of 15 May that marched on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-assembly-of-france">National Assembly</a> and was not released for ten years. He acquired the nickname L'Enfermé, the Prisoner.</p><p>On 14 August 1870 he led an abortive rising at La Villette, and on 17 March 1871, on the eve of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris-commune">Paris Commune</a>, he was arrested and held in Brittany. The Commune offered to exchange all its hostages, including the Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, for him alone, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adolphe-thiers">Adolphe Thiers</a> refused. He was freed in June 1879 and died of a stroke in Paris on 1 January 1881; a crowd of between one hundred thousand and two hundred thousand followed his funeral to Père Lachaise.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Blanqui separated the domain of nature, governed by immutable laws, from human affairs, which depend on will. He therefore rejected economic, religious and positivist determinism, a view often called political voluntarism. He held that poverty has ignorance as its main cause. In prison he wrote Eternity by the Stars in 1872. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-marx">Karl Marx</a> early called him the foremost leader of the French proletariat, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friedrich-engels">Friedrich Engels</a> later made Blanquism a pejorative term for conspiratorial putschism, which shaped his reputation.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Louis-Auguste Blanqui” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Commune's council, a mix of Jacobins, socialists and followers of Proudhon and <b>Blanqui</b>, separated church and state, abolished night work in bakeries, let workers take over abandoned workshops and postponed rents.</blockquote><a href="/state/paris-commune">Paris Commune</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Puget-Théniers, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=43.96,6.89&amp;year=1854" aria-label="Puget-Théniers on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4715 607 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Louis Auguste Blanqui"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5165 877h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5165 877h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=43.96,6.89&amp;year=1854">What was here in 1854? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
